we've worked in about 80 countries we've  worked with thousands of human rights  defenders and the aim is always to turn  a personal story of abuse into a  powerful tool for justice my name is  yvette albertine tame and i'm the  executive director of witness which is a  global human rights organization based  in brooklyn witness was started about 20  years ago by the activist and musician  peter gabriel he traveled on the amnesty  human rights first tour and he was  traveling in countries with a very big  old clunky video camera and he started  talking to people it suffered human  rights abuses or had seen members of  their family killed and he realized when  they filmed their story that once that  story was on film that it could no  longer be buried or forgotten and that's  what started witness and shortly after  that the Rodney King incident happens in  America which is where an innocent  bystander filmed the police beating up a  man and it all of a sudden put the  handycam on the map and with the  realization that video can be such a  powerful tool and it changes the  equation in terms of documents and you  rights violations witness was started so  what we do is we partner with grassroots  groups because they're the ones who know  the issues best and they know the  solutions myth and we support them in  understanding how to use video to  document their stories and then what  happens with these videos is we help  them think strategically about who are  the people who need to see that video  for their for change to happen so it's  always aimed at creating policy change  or behavior change some of the changes  that witness has actually been able to  effect in its partnership with a local  human rights group in the Congo was  aimed at stopping the mobilisation of  child soldiers Congo has gone through a  very brutal civil war and the militias  were recruiting children to become part  of the militias so what we did is we  worked with a local organization called  a JDK we had the children tell their  stories  what happened to them after they were  recruited and that video was used in  eastern Congo to go from village to  village together with community  organizers and show what happens to  these children's to children once they  became soldiers and it they it helped  really educate families because they  were they were volunteering their  children into war because they didn't  realize that it wasn't a good thing the  other way that video was used is to  lobby the International Criminal Court  based in The Hague in the Netherlands to  arrest Thomas Lubanga who was a is is a  Congolese warlords that was actually one  of the perpetrators who was recruiting  children into war and he's now sending  child at an International Criminal Court  that was a successful effort and that  was a shared success because obviously  we were not the only ones who were  working at it but it was a major success  technology is an incredibly powerful  tool when you put it in the hands of  people on the ground who are trying to  create human rights change twenty years  ago the dream that witness has what what  what if every human rights defender  would have a camera in their hands  what would they document and what could  they change and today with video and  equipped mobile phones and devices in  the hands of literally billions of  people anyone can be a human rights  defender think about the students in  Iran think about how they actually  filmed what was going on and these were  the stories that the Iranian government  which was trying to repress and the fact  that everybody has seen for example the  video of the girl made a sultan who was  one of the demonstrators being killed  made it impossible for that regime to  deny those stories the flip side of that  is that the people who are filming and  being filmed are also at more danger  because what's now happening is the  images of these people are showing up in  many different places and sometimes that  can actually expose people to a lot of  risk it's very dangerous for people to  to documents for you know it's it it's  one thing if you're in the US and you're  filming a police officer and that person  says you're not allowed to do that it's  another thing if you're in Burma and or  in Tibet and actually  being known to to speak out can can have  can be a matter of life and death in  terms of what continues to be a really  big challenge for us I would mention two  things one the demand for our services  is always much bigger than what we can  provide or a small organization  we're very effective but there are 30  people and there's always more groups  out there that we can work with then the  people that we can actually serve so  we're now starting to create digital  toolkits to get sort of these skill sets  out to more people so they can be  effective when they use video the other  thing I would say is that the world of  Human Rights governments and the  perpetrators are also becoming very  adept at using technology so sometimes  when you look at places like Burma or  Iran it can be really an uphill battle  to continue to fight for a freedom of  expression but the thing I do say is  there's always in the end there's more  of us so we you know I think every  single person can make difference
 

